{"id":"sole-source-new-firms","title":"No competition, and registered within the year","question":"Which suppliers got their first City contract without competition within a year of the earliest business start date registered under their name?","finding":"59 suppliers got a first City contract without competition within a year of their earliest registered business start date; their no-competition contracts since add up to $401.9 million. 10 of the first awards were made under emergency powers and 49 by sole source, ordinance waiver or another \"not bid\" rule. The largest: Color Health, Inc. (0 days, $84.4 million, emergency powers); BEUMER Lifecycle Management LLC (318 days, $65.3 million, sole source); SF New Deal (56 days, $58.9 million, emergency powers).","how":"The earliest start date on any Treasurer and Tax Collector business registration under the supplier’s exact name was compared with the start of its first contract in the Supplier Contracts table. Listed when that first contract was awarded without competition, began from 90 days before to 365 days after the registered start, and the supplier’s no-competition contracts total $100,000 or more. Joint ventures, partnerships and hotels are left out because they are formed per deal.","limits":"Name match on the exact normalized name. The registered start date is self-reported and is the date the firm began doing business in San Francisco, so a long-established national firm that registers when it wins its first City work looks new here. Pandemic programs were set up in days and are expected in the list. This extends the project’s 90-day screen to a year and to no-competition awards only; rows that also appear there are the same facts. The paid figures count payments issued in fiscal 2019 or later, which is read from the voucher number and not from the year the file lists a line under; $1.08 billion that the whole payment file lists under those years was issued earlier and is left out.","tier":"REDFLAG","colum